WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.741
contrary to this new movement about ideas
and dialogue and platforming and hearing everything

00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:07.741
everybody

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:08.741
out.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:12.790
I think it's important at some point to insert
what should be a pretty obvious reality to

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.690
us, which is that no matter what conversations
you want to have or you choose to have or

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:22.510
you enable the having of, you can't find middle
ground with crazy people.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.140
Not every idea deserves to be part of the
discussion.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:30.930
Not every person can be part of the solution
and some people and their ideas have to be

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:37.309
ignored, have to be kept out of the discussion
or we are going to truly damage ourselves

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:41.159
as a species, as a human society, as a country.

00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:42.800
However you want to slice and dice it.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.050
Now before people go crazy and start writing
in saying, David, you're calling for censorship.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.170
You're calling for mistreatment of people.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.190
You are limiting people's first amendment
rights and limiting what people have to say.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.820
I'm not okay.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.370
This came up last week during the interview
I did with Joe Rogan in Los Angeles.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.059
There's lots of great things about this movement
of the last several years that says it wants

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:10.409
to hear all ideas and it wants to figure out
what's best than have in depth discussion

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:14.350
was with people from all sides of the political
spectrum.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:15.350
That's great.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.350
Okay.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.820
I've been doing it for 10 years on the show.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:18.939
Dialogue is good.

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:20.840
Discourse is critical to human advancement.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:26.630
A diverse range of opinions should be heard
and considered and evaluated.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.729
When we are trying to figure out as humans
what is best for our future and what's best

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:37.539
for our societies, but there are people out
there with outrageously extreme bad ideas

00:01:37.539 --> 00:01:42.670
and it's okay to put the line somewhere and
say, you don't get a seat at the table.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:47.999
We're going to figure this thing out without
you because your ideas are terrible and crazy

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:49.689
and dangerous in some cases.

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:54.039
Now, of course the devil is in the details
and who is it that gates gets that sort of

00:01:54.039 --> 00:01:55.830
gatekeeping power?

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:00.350
That's a real question, but during the Joe
Rogan interview, I pointed out I'm one of

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.840
the biggest advocates of conversations with
people.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.289
We disagree with.

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:11.110
Respectful debate, the marketplace of ideas,
responsible platforming of extremism even,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:16.380
but that at a certain point you can't have
reasonable conversations with some people.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.010
I gave the example if we're trying to figure
out what the top marginal tax rate should

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:26.990
be in modern western countries like ours that
have economic systems that depend on government

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:33.630
revenue already, what role is there for someone
who thinks all taxes are government slavery,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:34.630
right?

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:39.920
You have to do the responsible and not give
that person a seat at the table when it comes

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.690
down to actually making policy, okay, you
want to do a TV show and have someone from

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.640
the taxes or slavery camp do it.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.740
You want to have an academic debate about
taxation, bring the taxes or slavery people,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:57.090
but when we're trying to figure this out in
the serious legislative sense, does that person

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.460
demand or or rather deserve a seat at the
table?

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.340
No, it's just an interruption.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.320
It's going to get in the way of actually figuring
things out when we're trying to figure out

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.580
the issue of it being legal still now in 2019
in a bunch of states to fire people based

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:17.090
on their sexual orientation, real or perceived
sexual orientation.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:23.110
Should a lawmaker or a voter who believes
that gay people should be put to death have

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.410
a seat at the table in that conversation?

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.410
No.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:27.540
It would be irresponsible.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.740
It would be ridiculous to give that person
a seat at the table.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.210
There's nothing wrong with this.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:33.210
Okay.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.580
It's called modern society in philosophy.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.540
There's something called The Hague alien dialectic.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:44.360
It's named after Hagle, a German philosopher
from the late 18th early 19th century, and

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:51.290
the basic idea of the huggy alien dialectic
is that the process of change comes from the

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:54.530
process of figuring things out, maybe is a
better way of saying it.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:04:01.900
It comes from a thesis and then an anti thesis
or an antithesis and then a synthesis of the

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.950
two into some kind of path forward.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:10.580
The underlying idea is that we're constantly
merging opposed ideologies that will get us

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:18.690
closer to an ideal or a solution or perfection
of some kind to use a utopian idealistic term.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:24.620
This doesn't work with outrageous, bad faith,
ignorant or cognitively limited people and

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:26.350
horrible ideas, period.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:33.210
We need to stop going so far into the marketplace
of ideas to the point where it becomes self

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.260
destructed by destructive.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.320
Rather.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.570
When we mandate that all ideas be considered
equally and given equal footing, we are actually

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.780
seeding ground to extremism.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.650
And this is not a hypothetical.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.860
This is how the right has won many victories.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:56.560
If eventually the solution is going to end
up as the midpoint or the average or the mean

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:03.180
of all ideas by presenting the most extreme
possible ideas, you pull the average to your

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.600
side.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.570
Republicans know this, the right node, this
knows this and they've used it successfully.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:14.160
If the debate over abortion, uh, is about
up until what point in a pregnancy should

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.070
abortion be legal?

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:21.139
And we sort of concede that we're going to
end up in the middle of all of the suggestions

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:22.220
that we get.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:28.050
The antiabortion right is incentivize to straight
up say all abortions should be illegal at

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.930
one day of pregnancy.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.240
Abortion is illegal.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:38.389
And if we guarantee that crazy position a
seat at the table, they win by suggesting

00:05:38.389 --> 00:05:43.280
the craziest possible idea and pulling the
Overton window, pulling the average of the

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.960
debate over to their side.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.860
If the debate over the highest marginal tax
rate is going to end up as the average of

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:56.950
all the proposed ideas, the right benefits
by saying the tax rate should be zero or taxation

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.840
is slavery because it pulls the average towards
them.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.020
I wished Democrats had understood this longer
ago if they had decided not to seed a ton

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.770
of ground to republicans trying to negotiate
over things that Republicans simply will not

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.330
negotiate about.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.960
We would be far better off today.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.940
Uh, if you do a show and you want to hear
out wacky ideas, do it.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:22.930
I do it on this show as long as I'm prepared
to responsibly do it, pushing back against

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:24.710
them, giving an opposing view.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:29.050
But when we're trying to have the serious
conversations, we don't have any responsibility

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.230
to let just any crazy old notion into the
discussion.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:36.290
When a new plane is being developed, there
is a cutoff as to what ideas of Boeing or

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:41.389
Airbus allow into the discussion and who is
allowed to contribute to the plains design.

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:42.389
Anything else?

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:45.830
We'd be ridiculous when we consider the best
treatment for a medical condition.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:48.520
Not every crazy notion is given the same value.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:49.860
This is logical.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.600
It shouldn't be different with politics.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.990
This isn't censorship.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.640
Everyone can say whatever they want.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:00.570
Say It on Youtube, go to your local city council
meeting and say it, write a letter, uh, to

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:05.970
your elected officials, do a podcast, whatever,
but you can't negotiate with crazy people

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:11.050
and their crazy ideas and they don't have
to be given a seat at the big boy table.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.050
And that's okay.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.050
Period.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:13.056
Let me know if you disagree.

